<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sextus Julius Frontinus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 40–103</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sextus Julius Frontinus was a Roman senator, general and writer. As governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> from about 74 to 78 he subdued the Silures of south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> fortress at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caerleon">Caerleon</a>. Later appointed curator of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">aqueducts</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, he wrote a treatise on the city's water supply, De aquaeductu, and a collection of military stratagems.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sextus-julius-frontinus.jpg" alt="Sextus Julius Frontinus" width="320" height="315" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sextus_Iulius_Frontinus_1788_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>No honorary or funerary inscription survives for Frontinus, so there is no outline of his life, and the names of his parents and his wife are unknown. Some details can be inferred from chance mentions. He is thought to have been of Narbonese origin and originally of the equestrian class. The name of Publius Calvisius Ruso Julius Frontinus, consul about 84, suggests that Frontinus had a sister who was that man's mother. He had at least one daughter, who married Quintus Sosius Senecio and was the mother of Sosia Polla.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 70 CE Frontinus took part in the suppression of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> revolt, and he later recorded that he received the surrender of 70,000 Lingones. He was appointed suffect consul before being sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to succeed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-petillius-cerialis">Quintus Petillius Cerialis</a> as governor. There he subjugated the Silures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-wales">South Wales</a> and is thought to have campaigned against the Brigantes as well. His successor was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Gnaeus Julius Agricola</a>, the father-in-law of the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>. An inscription at Hieropolis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> and coins of Smyrna attest that he was proconsul of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> in 86.</p><p>In 97 the emperor Nerva appointed him curator aquarum, supervisor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">aqueducts</a>, an office given only to men of very high standing. The next year he held a second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a> as suffect, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> as his colleague, and two years later he was consul ordinarius with Trajan, an honour that the historian Birley takes as a sign of the high regard in which he was held. He was also a member of the College of Augurs, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-younger">Pliny the Younger</a> wrote that he was elected to fill the vacancy Frontinus' death had created.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His chief work, De aquaeductu, is an official report to the emperor in two books. It gives the history, sizes and discharge rates of the nine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">aqueducts</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in his day and sets out the laws governing their use and maintenance. As water commissioner he first had maps of the system prepared in order to judge its condition. He found that many channels were neglected and was concerned by farmers and tradesmen who tapped the supply with pipes; lead pipe stamps bearing the owner's name were used against such theft. He also separated the waters of different systems rather than mixing them, and warned that tree roots could damage the masonry.</p><p>He also wrote a treatise on military science, which is lost. His surviving military work, the Stratagems, collects examples of stratagems from Greek and Roman history for the use of generals. It draws on his own experience as a general in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a>, though similarities with Valerius Maximus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> suggest he relied mainly on literary sources. The authenticity of its fourth book has been challenged.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frontinus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Julius Frontinus</b> finally subdued them around 74 to 78 and founded the legionary fortress of Isca at Caerleon.</blockquote><a href="/state/silures">Silures</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis">Gallia Narbonensis</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44,4&amp;year=80" aria-label="Gallia Narbonensis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4646 605 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sextus Julius Frontinus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5096 875h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5096 875h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44,4&amp;year=80">What was here in 80 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
